# Primes and the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, modeled by the kernel. A PRIME is a number greater than one whose only
# divisors are one and itself; a COMPOSITE factors into smaller pieces. The kernel exhibits the factorizations of the
# composites (4 = 2 times 2, 6 = 2 times 3, and so on), the early primes 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, the TWIN PRIMES (pairs two
# apart), and an instance of the FUNDAMENTAL THEOREM OF ARITHMETIC: every number is a unique product of primes (12 = 2
# times 2 times 3, 100 = 2 times 2 times 5 times 5). Primes are the multiplicative atoms of the integers, the building
# blocks all of number theory and cryptography rest on.

# FACTORIZATIONS of the small composites: 4 = 2 times 2.
hold four-is-two-times-two
  call is-equal
    code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 2

hold six-is-two-times-three
  call is-equal
    code 6
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 3

hold nine-is-three-times-three
  call is-equal
    code 9
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 3

# A prime CANNOT be split this way: there is no pair of factors above one. Five sits strictly between the products that
# bracket it, 2 times 2 = 4 below and 2 times 3 = 6 above, so no multiple of two lands on it; it is prime.
hold five-is-between-four-and-six
  call is-above
    code 5
    code 4

hold five-is-below-six
  call is-below
    code 5
    code 6

# TWIN PRIMES, pairs two apart: (3, 5), (5, 7), (11, 13). Each gap is exactly two, 5 = 3 + 2, 7 = 5 + 2, 13 = 11 + 2.
hold twin-three-five
  call is-equal
    code 5
    call add
      code 3
      code 2

hold twin-eleven-thirteen
  call is-equal
    code 13
    call add
      code 11
      code 2

# THE FUNDAMENTAL THEOREM OF ARITHMETIC, an instance: twelve factors uniquely as 2 times 2 times 3.
hold twelve-factors-into-primes
  call is-equal
    code 12
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 3

# A larger instance: one hundred is 2 times 2 times 5 times 5, the unique prime factorization.
hold hundred-factors-into-primes
  call is-equal
    code 100
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 5
          code 5
